      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14257398/assets/1320b407942e9c1c1a2cad98517e9700946adfab1e3929d51b70b63151545f22.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{6}x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{6}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $6$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $6.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14257398/assets/611f0de76f6eedd063af38f85f53d4948aeba52d2019223d08000f1aa95b0cc5.png)",
